---

## What We Are Building

NEXORA is a high-craft cyberpunk & indie game distribution marketplace built with Angular 18+ (standalone components, reactive Signals, OnPush change detection, interface-driven DI, and strict Steam DesignMD styling).

In this session, we perfected the responsive mobile layout (single-hero display without duplicate cover artwork stacking), engineered a persistent Steam-style bottom Download Tray (`DownloadService`), implemented universal accessible `<kbd>Escape</kbd>` modal dismissal, and eliminated all touch scrolling friction on iOS Safari via WebKit kinetic momentum isolation (`touch-action: pan-y`, dedicated GPU compositing layer, and RAF scroll scheduling). We also configured and guided Cloudflare Tunnel (`cloudflared`) live proxy testing for real-time mobile dogfooding.

---

## What WORKED (with evidence)

- **Mobile Single-Hero Layout**: Removed duplicate cover image on mobile viewports (`display: none` at `< 900px`) in [`game-detail.component.css`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/features/game-detail/game-detail.component.css) while enabling touch-friendly screenshot carousel scrolling (`-webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch`).
- **Steam Global Bottom Download Tray**: Created [`DownloadService`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/core/services/download.service.ts), [`download-tray.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/shared/ui/download-tray/download-tray.component.ts), and integrated into [`app.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/app.component.ts). Verified with active download progress simulation (`0% -> 38% -> 76% -> 100%`) and `[ Play ]` direct launch actions.
- **Universal Category Rail Edge Fade Masks**: Standardized `mask-image: linear-gradient(...)` across Library, Wishlist, and Catalog filter tracks.
- **Universal Accessible Escape Dismissal**: Added HostListener Escape key dismissal across all modal dialogs ([`orders.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/features/orders/orders.component.ts), [`library.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/features/library/library.component.ts), [`wishlist.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/features/wishlist/wishlist.component.ts), [`creator-studio.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/features/creator-studio/creator-studio.component.ts)).
- **Hardware-Accelerated & iOS 120Hz ProMotion Scrolling**: Wrapped `window:scroll` calculations with `requestAnimationFrame` in [`app.component.ts`](file:///c:/Users/User/Downloads/AngularProject/src/app/app.component.ts), isolated mobile drawer to a single scroll layer (`overflow: hidden` on drawer, `overflow-y: scroll; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch !important; touch-action: pan-y !important` on nav list), and bypassed synthetic CSS smooth scrolling on iOS WebKit devices via `@supports (-webkit-touch-callout: none)`.
- **Automated Verification Gate**: Confirmed 100% pass rate across all test suites via `npm run verify` (401 unit tests, 51 integration tests, 10 master battery tests, 7 impeccable anti-slop rules).

---

## What Did NOT Work (and why)

- **Global `html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }` on iOS Safari**: Caused rubbery friction and micro-stutters because WebKit's native 120Hz ProMotion touch deceleration engine was fighting synthetic CSS scroll interpolation. Resolved by scoping smooth scrolling off on WebKit touch devices (`scroll-behavior: auto !important`).
- **Nested `overflow-y: auto` in Mobile Drawer**: Caused double-scroll momentum contention on touch screens. Resolved by setting `.mobile-drawer { overflow: hidden; }` and keeping `.mobile-nav` as the single dedicated scroll container.

